Women's Mental Wellbeing, Safety and Protection in Tanzania
Organization
Arise and Rejoice Assisting Hands Organization
Project Overview
This proposed community-based initiative aims to promote the mental wellbeing, safety, and protection of women and girls in Tanzania, particularly those affected by gender-based violence, discrimination, stigma, and social exclusion.
The Challenge
Some women and girls experience violence, emotional distress, discrimination, and difficulties accessing appropriate support services. Limited awareness of available services and fear of stigma can make it difficult to seek help.
Proposed Solution
The initiative proposes to:
•	Conduct community awareness sessions on mental wellbeing, safety, protection, and women's rights.
•	Increase awareness of gender-based violence prevention and available support services.
•	Encourage safe, respectful community discussions that reduce stigma.
•	Connect women and girls with appropriate healthcare, psychosocial support, and protection services through qualified providers.
•	Work with relevant community stakeholders to strengthen referral pathways.
Intended Beneficiaries
Women and girls in local communities in Tanzania, especially those experiencing vulnerability, discrimination, or barriers to accessing support.
Current Development Status
The initiative is at the proposal and planning stage. A dedicated pilot focused on mental wellbeing, safety, and protection has not yet been completed. The project will require funding, partnerships, safeguarding procedures, and monitoring arrangements before implementation.
Planned Next Steps
1.	Consult women and community stakeholders to understand local needs.
2.	Develop educational materials and safeguarding procedures.
3.	Establish referral arrangements with relevant service providers.
4.	Conduct a small pilot when resources and appropriate partnerships are in place.
5.	Evaluate results and improve the program based on participant feedback.
Transparency
This repository documents the proposed initiative and its development plans. It does not claim that a digital product has been built or that the proposed health initiative has already been tested.

